WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.539
Behind me on the launchpad is

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.540
Artemis one.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.169
It's the first step in NASA's

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.819
ambitious program to bring American

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.259
astronauts back to the moon for the

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.179
first time in half a century

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.740
and then on to Mars.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.769
Assuming NASA can pull it off, which

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.359
is a big question mark, is the

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.979
project worth hundreds of billions

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.119
of government money?

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.709
Is NASA just trying to relive the

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.449
glory of the Apollo mission?

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.979
Will private companies take us back

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.959
to the Moon and Mars, faster and

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.069
cheaper?

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.599
Artemis is the latest in a long

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.339
series of setbacks and reversals in

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.049
America's space program.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.879
In 2010, President Obama

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:36.809
announced the shelving of the

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.459
Constellation program, which was

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.899
NASA's last attempt to bring

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.609
astronauts back to the moon.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.439
The following year, the agency shut

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.939
down its space shuttle program

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.349
altogether because of costs and

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.789
safety concerns.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.409
Obama also announced that NASA's

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.939
would begin relying more on private

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.139
contractors.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.879
I recognize that some have said it

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.739
is unfeasible or unwise to work

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.090
with the private sector in this way.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.289
I disagree.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.819
But six years later, the Trump

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.409
administration greenlit the Artemis

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:04.999
program, which was heralded as the

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.889
beginning of a new space exploration

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.869
renaissance at NASA.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.429
It marks an important step in

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.709
returning American astronauts

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.809
to the moon for the first time

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.879
since 1972.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.709
This directive will ensure America's

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.629
space program once again leads and

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.120
inspires all of humanity.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.159
The pioneer spirit has always

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.019
defined America.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.909
Artemis one, the unmanned test

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.619
route that traveled 1.4 million

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.599
miles through space, splashed down

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.829
on December 12th.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.329
And that's just the first of four

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.449
planned launches.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.059
Artemus II will bring humans into

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.189
space and Artemis III, scheduled

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.049
to launch in 2025, is supposed

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.249
to bring humans to the moon with

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.289
help from Space X's starship

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.069
vehicle.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.109
After that, NASA's planning nearly

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.909
Artemis launches.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.009
And liftoff of Artemis one.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.839
We rise together back

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.329
to the moon and beyond.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:57.560
That's the plan.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.499
Artemis I's first three attempts at

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.909
liftoff were canceled because of

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.199
leaking issues connected to the

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.849
rocket's crown system.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.279
The Artemis I mission postponed

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.899
for the second time this week due to

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:09.900
technical issues.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.529
The rocket finally went airborne on

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.689
November 16, 2022.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.389
The price tag was $4.1 billion,

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.559
and the next three launches are

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.029
expected to each cost about the

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.189
same. NASA's auditor estimates

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.449
that the Artemis program will spend

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.599
$93 billion by 2025.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.709
Because of delays.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:29.329
It's unlikely that a human will make

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:30.649
it back to the moon by the end of

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.719
2024, as originally planned.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.704
From 1960 to 1973

00:02:35.705 --> 00:02:37.909
NASA's spent 280

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.889
billion in today's dollars on

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.989
moon landings, but the agency

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:43.279
remains one of America's most

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.439
popular, according to opinion polls,

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.706
alongside the National Park Service

00:02:46.707 --> 00:02:48.469
and, surprisingly, the U.S.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.359
Postal Service.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.799
Beating the Soviets to the moon

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:52.939
bolstered America's claim to

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.589
technological and ideological

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.739
superiority in the 20th century.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.909
That's one small step for man,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.619
one giant leap for

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.620
mankind.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.339
But ever since Neil Armstrong's one

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.019
small step, there have been giant

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.909
leaps in space transportation made

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.429
in large part by the private sector.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:15.169
Oh, wow.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.119
Liftoff of the satellite

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.120
and crew Dragon. Go NASA, go SpaceX.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.039
In 2009, the private sector saw

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.839
one of its biggest champions ascend

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.249
to become the number two person at

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.349
NASA. Lori Garver pushed to describe

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.029
the Constellation program as a way

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.949
to entice the private sector to fill

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:34.819
in the gaps.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.439
She also spearheaded the commercial

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.179
crew program, which continues to

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:39.769
employ commercial contractors to

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:40.909
ferry astronauts to the

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.099
International Space Station.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.929
Today, companies like Elon Musk's

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.459
SpaceX Are launching rockets at a

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.319
faster pace and for a fraction

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.269
of what NASA's spends.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.799
"What we're seeing in short is the

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:53.419
beginning of a historic transfer of

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.159
space operations from the public to

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.869
the private sector," wrote then

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:58.699
Reason editor Bob Poole.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.469
That was in the magazine's April

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.999
1979 issue.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.529
Poole, who has always been ahead of

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.179
the curve on issues related to space

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.159
and privatization, is now Reason

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.389
Foundation's director of

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.219
transportation policy.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.409
In the magazine's December 2022

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.059
issue, he wrote about how science

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.859
fiction writer Robert Heinlein's

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.719
dream of private space travel is

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:18.859
coming true.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.299
Commercial space transport is the

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.459
obvious future, and I think we're

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.019
we're getting well on the way to it.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.269
After the Artemis program.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.249
Former politician and current Nassau

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.139
chief Bill Nelson argues that space

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.849
travel is a function that must be

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.439
carried out by the government.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.629
Space is a very unforgiving

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.849
kind of environment, and NASA

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:40.489
has always run

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.529
the programs

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.619
being built by contractors,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.809
not contractors running

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.459
the programs.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.469
With oversight of NASA.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:52.789
Now that's a new way of doing

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:53.389
business.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.009
Back when Nelson made those remarks,

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.629
commercial space transport was still

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:57.529
nascent.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.299
More than a decade later, private

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.409
space companies have proven

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.269
themselves to be more competent and

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.099
cost effective than NASA.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.659
Does NASA still need to be in the

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.609
space travel business at all?

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.439
The moon is really a stepping stone.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.209
We really need to learn how to live

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.829
and work on the moon before we ever

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:13.649
go to Mars.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.319
And that's the whole reason of the

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:16.622
Artemis program.

00:05:16.623 --> 00:05:18.619
Lily Villareal is a NASA engineer

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:20.119
who works on the Artemis team.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:22.009
She says that exploring the cosmos

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:23.539
is critical to the long term

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.219
preservation of the human race,

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.199
making it a valid use of public

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.099
funds.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.079
I really think it's important to go

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:31.579
to deep space and learn how to live

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.199
in deep space because we have

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.629
limited resources here on Earth.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.519
We eventually are going to

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.409
become a society where we have

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.269
to move beyond

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.979
Earth to be able to live.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:44.839
She says that Artemis' price tag

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.669
is justified by the need for NASA

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.529
to be safe and thorough.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.299
This is the first time we are

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.349
actually putting this rocket

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.539
together, and so we always expected

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:54.349
challenges.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:55.879
One of the things about NASA's we

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:56.869
test like you fly.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:58.019
We want to make sure that we get it.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.969
Right. Because this you know, we

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:01.439
want this wants to be successful.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.519
It's a test flight.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.869
So we're doing everything possible

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.219
that we can to make sure that we are

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.139
ready to go. And really we won't go

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.999
into we are ready to go.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.619
The biggest criticism of the

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.599
project. Is the cost.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:13.979
How do you respond to that type of

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:14.649
criticism?

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:17.309
It's an investment in

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.229
to our civilization about

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.179
our ability to learn how to go

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.889
to deep space and live in deep

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.599
space, which is something right now

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.059
we don't have the capability to do.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:28.769
I believe that this nation should

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.779
commit itself to achieving

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:32.669
the goal before this

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.799
decade is out of landing

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.719
a man on the moon and returning him

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:37.800
safely to the earth.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.799
No single space project in this

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.989
period will be more impressive

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.299
to mankind, or more important

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.069
for the long range exploration of

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:48.239
space.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.309
And none will be so difficult

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:51.689
or expensive to accomplish.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.659
The space race was also a way of

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.159
demonstrating America's military

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.169
dominance over the Soviet Union,

00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.639
which had embarrassed the U.S.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.979
with the 1957 launch of Sputnik,

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.419
the first artificial Earth

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.249
satellite, NASA and its

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.749
political backers seem determined to

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.219
recapture the glory of the Apollo

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.719
missions, with Artemis.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.519
Poole says that the agency tends to

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.810
stick to old ways of doing things

00:07:12.811 --> 00:07:14.759
isn't particularly interested

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.499
in cost savings and its decision

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.139
making is overly driven by politics.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.669
Basically, it's designed to repeat

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.049
the Apollo program.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:23.849
The hardware that powered Artemus I

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.829
is known as the SLS, the Space

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.659
Launch System, and was developed in

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.069
partnership with Boeing.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.765
Critics have another name for it.

00:07:30.766 --> 00:07:32.579
The Senate Launch System, because it

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.409
was concocted by the Senate as a

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.419
way to rescue Constellation, give

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.589
it a new name.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:38.969
Paul says NASA would have been

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.679
better off replacing the costly and

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.419
dated space launch system used in

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:43.799
the Artemis program.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.509
But it didn't, which probably has a

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:46.709
lot to do with the fact that it was

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.209
largely constructed and engineered

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.159
in Alabama, the home state of Senate

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.169
Appropriations Chair Richard Shelby,

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.579
who has a history of strong arming

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.049
NASA's to preserve jobs for his

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.519
constituents.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.379
In 2019, the Senate earmarked

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:00.269
$2.58 billion

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.069
as a starting point to cover the

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.509
space launch systems ballooning

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:04.529
price tag.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.149
The Senate funding also required

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.039
NASA to make use of old warehouse

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.719
materials from Constellation,

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:12.239
ostensibly a cost saving measure.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:13.859
Well, it turns out after sitting in

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.659
the warehouse, each one has to be

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.789
refurbished at about 150

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.379
million per engine.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.389
There's four of them in SLS.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.369
And so it's $600 million just

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.829
to refurbish the used

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.809
all tech engines for every

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.249
single launch.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.229
For four years in a row under two

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:32.489
different administrations.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.589
NASA's got more money from Congress,

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.149
then the president requested.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.219
The human spaceflight program

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.229
of this country can only be

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.119
led by the elected leader of

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.038
this country because he set the

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.959
priorities, he is going

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.119
to have to

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.120
pony up,

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:51.929
inhibit his Office of

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.459
the Management and Budget, more

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:54.479
money.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.069
The biggest boondoggle in the

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.629
industry right now is the space

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:58.799
launch system.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.839
The Constellation program and

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.369
the space shuttle were both canceled

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:03.839
around 2011.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.119
And senators in Alabama,

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:07.829
Texas and Florida wanted to keep the

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:09.719
jobs associated with those programs

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.279
even after the cancelations.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:12.899
And so they created a derivative

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.759
rocket program and told NASA to

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:15.719
do it.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.339
Eli Dourado is an economist at the

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.899
Center for Growth and Opportunity at

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.999
Utah State University, who studies

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.619
regulation of the commercial space

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.609
industry.

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.679
He says the SLS is largely a jobs

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.509
program that utilizes wasteful

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.429
cost plus contracts that

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.079
require NASA to fully cover a

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.209
project's expenses, plus an extra

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.339
fee as profit, eliminating any

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.859
risk taken on by the contractor.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.749
There's no incentive to find

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.159
cost savings.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.259
NASA's Bill Nelson has called cost

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:45.209
plus contracting "a plague."

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.219
Dourado says NASA's entire rocket

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.569
program should be outsourced to

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.279
private companies like SpaceX and

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.199
Blue Origin, who bid to complete the

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.419
work at a fixed cost.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:55.799
The commercial approach

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:57.910
is to

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.289
create a market for the service

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.149
that you want to buy

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:05.399
and let hungry competitors

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.019
work to get the cost down and the

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:08.819
performance up.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.009
NASA's has some smart

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:13.169
scientists and some smart engineers,

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:14.969
but it's a huge bureaucracy and it's

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.009
very, very risk averse.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:18.749
Paul refers to NASA's cumbersome

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.399
rocket design process as the one

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.559
best way approach, in contrast

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.089
to private firms, which are always

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.739
on the lookout for more innovative

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.569
and cost effective ways of doing

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.579
things. SpaceX has engineered

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.349
a way to land its boosters after use

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:32.789
instead of disposing of them into

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.919
the ocean, as NASA does.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.509
Paul remembers the first time he saw

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.969
Space X's method in action.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.429
There came the boosters coming down

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.409
beautifully, settling down

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.779
right on target pads.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.159
And I thought, 'Oh my God, this is

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.899
incredible.' And the thought that

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:50.369
immediately came to mind was, 'My

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:52.559
God, Robert Heinlein should be alive

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.029
today to see this'.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.009
He wrote the first science fiction

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.656
story ever that I know of about

00:10:57.657 --> 00:10:59.869
private enterprise, creating

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.059
a rocket vehicle to launch

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.129
boom crystallized

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:06.169
me how important the business

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:07.879
approach to space launch has become.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.319
Space X has recovered 32 boosters

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.089
that have all been reused at least

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:14.919
one time for the Falcon nine and

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.719
Falcon heavy launch vehicles.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.699
One booster has even flown 14

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:19.599
times.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:20.889
The company is pushing to get that

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:22.809
number up to 30 while

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.909
Artimus one's launch costs $4

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:26.979
billion. The average launch of space

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:28.959
x's falcon heavy runs between 100

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:30.999
and 150 million, with enough

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:32.829
capacity to carry about 80%

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:34.179
of Artimus' load.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.009
It seems that SpaceX is still on

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:37.389
their way to perfecting that

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:39.429
reusability element for

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:40.359
rocket boosters.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:41.709
But the SLS system, like you said,

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.149
is not reusable.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.279
Why did NASA go that route of less

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:46.299
reusability?

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:48.219
The trade was the decision was we

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.539
want to get to the moon as fast as

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:50.919
possible. And for that, you need a

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:52.929
really big rocket for you

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.339
to get the thrust that you need.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:55.989
You just can't put the reusable

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.729
components into the vehicle to be

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:59.739
able to recover it afterwards.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.629
NASA's has contracted with SpaceX X

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:03.549
for the Artemis program with plans

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.349
to use its starship launch vehicle

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:07.209
still in development to take humans

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:08.739
from a lunar outpost to the moon's

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.839
surface and eventually on to Mars.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:13.959
Do you think NASA's role is always

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.939
going to be to build and launch

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.559
rockets, or is that going to be

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.449
outsourced, so to speak, to

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:20.679
other companies that seem to be

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.689
doing it quicker and more often?

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:24.249
I think we would definitely be very

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.319
much a part of launching rockets

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.119
and building rockets. For sure the

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:30.129
Artemis Campaign for the Future has

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:31.839
us continuing to launch SLS

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.399
vehicles. So you got to think about

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.349
the whole Artimus campaign is

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:37.209
not just the SLS,

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:39.249
it's also all of the other

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.809
components that come with it.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:42.189
Commercial companies supplying US

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:43.539
logistics or commercial companies

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:44.979
supplying US human landers,

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:46.869
supplying US deep space habitats.

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:48.819
And I think it's a good thing to

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.489
have a good combination

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:53.499
of our ability to go into space

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:55.929
and not make it all be one specific

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:57.249
way of doing it.

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.139
NASA already relies heavily on the

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:00.639
private sector, with private

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:02.319
companies designing and leasing the

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.209
agency much of its hardware.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:05.709
But Poole says there's no reason

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.359
NASA can't take it a step further

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:09.429
and just use the space X Starship

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.109
to cover the entire journey from

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:12.969
Earth to the moon and eventually

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:13.719
Mars.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:15.789
If the current NASA plan goes

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:17.739
ahead to have the

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:19.749
SpaceX starship actually

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:21.969
deliver the astronauts

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:24.039
from the lunar outpost in orbit

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:25.419
to the surface of the moon and bring

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:26.529
them back up.

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:29.049
That will be an even more dramatic

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.029
refutation of the idea that the

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:32.709
only NASA's should be doing space

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:34.239
transportation.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:35.829
Poole says that instead of flying

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.629
its own missions, NASA should play a

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:40.239
more limited and supportive role.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.129
The future NASA role that that

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:43.959
makes the best sense is

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:45.489
research and development in

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:46.690
advancing science,

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.869
helping with the development

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:52.299
of new orbital

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:53.590
telescopes and things.

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:56.739
Doing research that will

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.629
assist the private

00:13:58.630 --> 00:13:59.630
companies.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:02.709
Visiting the Kennedy Space Center,

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.119
it's easy to be nostalgic for the

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:05.979
glory days of the space program,

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:07.389
but the effort to relive them with

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:10.239
Artemis has been a costly mistake.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:11.919
Commercial space travel is the next

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:13.539
frontier, and it may be coming

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:14.919
sooner than we think.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:16.509
You look at what's going on in the

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:18.669
private and there are more private

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:20.829
launch startups of all sizes

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:22.459
going on, raising venture capital

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.059
today between the Defense Department

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.579
having successes with with launches

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.409
from Space X and presumably

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.239
other other companies.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.159
Space transportation

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.529
should be within

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:36.530
a decade or so, it's

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:40.539
possible to be the the main

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.489
model for how we get

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:44.349
people and things into space.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:46.179
And that would be an incredible

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:47.679
sea change.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:49.689
I'm telling you, we've never seen

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:51.909
such a tail

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:53.019
of flame.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:54.969
And I have to say,

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:57.789
for what we saw tonight,

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:00.219
it's an A-plus.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:02.589
Artemis one successfully launched.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:03.939
How did you feel watching the

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:04.940
launch?

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:07.629
Mixed feelings.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.399
The fact that it is a success, I

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:10.569
mean, assuming it completes the

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.729
mission and makes success,

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:13.730
then they plan

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:16.539
eight missions of this, which

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:18.789
means, you know, the 93 billion

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:20.919
only covers through 2024.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:22.839
So the cost of this is going to be

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:24.699
several hundred billion if this

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:25.989
all goes through.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.239
And so if there was a big failure

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.069
on the very first launch, there

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:31.389
would have been a chance to say,

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:33.439
well, look, all right, we

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:34.449
we we did this.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:35.349
We built it.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:36.249
It worked.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:38.169
But we really don't want to do eight

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.269
of these. But to commit to eight

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:42.279
all eight launches now strikes

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:44.649
me as very foolish decision.

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:45.939
If there's one thing to be learned

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:47.979
from the history of technology, it's

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:49.299
that government support entails

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:50.409
hidden perils.

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:52.869
Paul wrote in Reason back in 1979

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:54.429
when heralding the dawn of private

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:56.469
ventures in space, like

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:58.089
with industries such as railroads,

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:00.219
cars, telecommunications, TV

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:01.719
and the power grid, space

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:03.639
exploration saw new dynamism once

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:05.409
the government backed off.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:06.999
In the 50 years since NASA's

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.251
astronauts last walked on the moon,

00:16:08.252 --> 00:16:10.149
remarkable progress made

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:11.469
by the private sector shows that

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:14.109
Poole was right back in 1979.

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:16.089
We can and should develop

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:17.379
space without government help.

